WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.343
Lesson three point four.

2
00:00:01.669 --> 00:00:03.840
How a model with a memory is trained on

3
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.034
songs cut into chunks.

4
00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.239
Lesson one point three: live, the drums

5
00:00:11.239 --> 00:00:13.105
for slot t are decided before slot t's

6
00:00:13.105 --> 00:00:14.158
guitar has been heard.

7
00:00:14.445 --> 00:00:15.880
There is no future to look at.

8
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:18.416
So the model has to be causal: its output

9
00:00:18.416 --> 00:00:20.474
at slot t may use its inputs at slot t and

10
00:00:20.474 --> 00:00:21.527
before, never after.

11
00:00:21.814 --> 00:00:23.680
And its input at slot t is already the

12
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.737
guitar of slot t minus one, the one-slot

13
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:27.173
shift of lesson one point two.

14
00:00:27.614 --> 00:00:29.445
The slots after t are simply not there

15
00:00:29.445 --> 00:00:29.632
yet.

16
00:00:31.121 --> 00:00:33.329
The recurrent models, the LSTM and S4D,

17
00:00:33.329 --> 00:00:35.185
don't re-read the past at every slot.

18
00:00:35.486 --> 00:00:37.744
They carry a summary of it forward: the

19
00:00:37.744 --> 00:00:38.647
hidden state, h.

20
00:00:38.948 --> 00:00:41.106
At each slot it takes the old state, h t

21
00:00:41.106 --> 00:00:43.414
minus one, mixes in the new input, x t,

22
00:00:43.414 --> 00:00:45.370
and the result is the new state, h t.

23
00:00:45.671 --> 00:00:47.779
The nine drum outputs for slot t are read

24
00:00:47.779 --> 00:00:48.230
from h t.

25
00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:50.791
What f and g are is each model's own

26
00:00:50.791 --> 00:00:52.975
machinery, which topic four opens up.

27
00:00:53.311 --> 00:00:55.719
The transformer works differently: it

28
00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:58.351
keeps the past itself, up to a window, and

29
00:00:58.351 --> 00:01:00.423
looks back over all of it every slot.

30
00:01:01.955 --> 00:01:04.315
Each of our three model families carries a

31
00:01:04.315 --> 00:01:05.193
different state.

32
00:01:05.522 --> 00:01:08.046
Topic four opens the boxes; here, only

33
00:01:08.046 --> 00:01:08.705
their sizes.

34
00:01:09.034 --> 00:01:11.558
The LSTM carries two vectors, h and c, of

35
00:01:11.558 --> 00:01:13.698
a hundred and twenty-eight numbers for

36
00:01:13.698 --> 00:01:16.222
each of its two layers: five hundred and

37
00:01:16.222 --> 00:01:17.045
twelve numbers.

38
00:01:17.374 --> 00:01:19.844
S4D carries, in each of its four layers,

39
00:01:19.844 --> 00:01:22.094
thirty-two complex numbers for each of a

40
00:01:22.094 --> 00:01:24.727
hundred and twenty-eight channels: sixteen

41
00:01:24.727 --> 00:01:26.867
thousand three hundred and eighty-four

42
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:29.172
complex numbers, so thirty-two thousand

43
00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:31.532
seven hundred and sixty-eight real ones,

44
00:01:31.532 --> 00:01:33.342
since each complex number is two.

45
00:01:33.672 --> 00:01:35.921
The transformer's state is its key-value

46
00:01:35.921 --> 00:01:38.665
cache: for each of two layers, a key and a

47
00:01:38.665 --> 00:01:40.860
value for each of the last two thousand

48
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.000
and forty-seven slots, four heads of

49
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.372
twenty-four numbers each.

50
00:01:44.701 --> 00:01:47.115
Full, that's seven hundred and eighty-six

51
00:01:47.115 --> 00:01:48.926
thousand and forty-eight numbers.

52
00:01:49.397 --> 00:01:52.141
The LSTM's and S4D's states keep one size

53
00:01:52.141 --> 00:01:52.729
all song.

54
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.994
The cache grows by one slot each slot, up

55
00:01:55.994 --> 00:01:58.868
to two thousand and forty-seven, then the

56
00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.305
oldest slot drops off.

57
00:02:03.933 --> 00:02:06.362
Lesson two point eight cut every song into

58
00:02:06.362 --> 00:02:08.790
chunks of five hundred and twelve slots,

59
00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:10.089
one training step each.

60
00:02:10.428 --> 00:02:12.687
The median Lakh song is sixteen hundred

61
00:02:12.687 --> 00:02:15.229
slots long, so it spans four chunks, the

62
00:02:15.229 --> 00:02:16.584
last one mostly padding.

63
00:02:17.079 --> 00:02:19.218
The state at the end of chunk zero isn't

64
00:02:19.218 --> 00:02:19.845
thrown away.

65
00:02:20.158 --> 00:02:21.984
It's carried into chunk one as its

66
00:02:21.984 --> 00:02:24.541
starting state, and on, chunk to chunk, to

67
00:02:24.541 --> 00:02:25.585
the end of the song.

68
00:02:25.898 --> 00:02:27.829
It starts fresh only when a new song

69
00:02:27.829 --> 00:02:30.386
starts, at the batch's first chunk: zeros

70
00:02:30.386 --> 00:02:32.631
for the LSTM and S4D, an empty cache for

71
00:02:32.631 --> 00:02:33.466
the transformer.

72
00:02:33.901 --> 00:02:36.160
In the training loop: on a song's first

73
00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:37.315
chunk, a fresh state.

74
00:02:37.616 --> 00:02:39.624
Otherwise the model is handed the state

75
00:02:39.624 --> 00:02:40.829
the last chunk returned.

76
00:02:42.436 --> 00:02:44.136
Does memory really cross the edge?

77
00:02:44.436 --> 00:02:46.386
Take a real song from lesson two point

78
00:02:46.386 --> 00:02:48.836
eight: seven hundred and four slots, so a

79
00:02:48.836 --> 00:02:50.736
full chunk zero and a short chunk one.

80
00:02:51.036 --> 00:02:52.986
Zero the onset flag at input slot five

81
00:02:52.986 --> 00:02:55.036
hundred, in chunk zero, which is the

82
00:02:55.036 --> 00:02:56.636
guitar of slot four hundred and

83
00:02:56.636 --> 00:02:58.986
ninety-nine, and run the song again, state

84
00:02:58.986 --> 00:03:00.136
carried as in training.

85
00:03:00.588 --> 00:03:02.504
The drum probabilities in chunk one

86
00:03:02.504 --> 00:03:04.952
change: by up to six thousandths for the

87
00:03:04.952 --> 00:03:07.348
LSTM, L thirty-two, and three hundredths

88
00:03:07.348 --> 00:03:09.583
for the transformer, T one twenty-eight.

89
00:03:09.902 --> 00:03:12.510
Small, but not zero: what it heard before

90
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.799
the edge still changes what it plays after

91
00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:14.959
it.

92
00:03:18.423 --> 00:03:20.474
Lesson three point three: training needs

93
00:03:20.474 --> 00:03:22.347
the gradient, how the loss changes with

94
00:03:22.347 --> 00:03:24.308
each weight, and backpropagation finds it

95
00:03:24.308 --> 00:03:25.913
by running the chain rule backwards.

96
00:03:26.181 --> 00:03:28.098
With a carried state, the loss at slot t

97
00:03:28.098 --> 00:03:30.104
depends on h t, which depends on h t minus

98
00:03:30.104 --> 00:03:32.111
one, and so on, back to the start of the

99
00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:32.334
song.

100
00:03:32.601 --> 00:03:34.429
So the gradient flows back through every

101
00:03:34.429 --> 00:03:35.053
earlier state.

102
00:03:35.321 --> 00:03:36.926
That's backpropagation through time.

103
00:03:37.997 --> 00:03:40.364
How much does the loss at slot t blame the

104
00:03:40.364 --> 00:03:42.015
state at an earlier slot, k?

105
00:03:42.346 --> 00:03:44.492
Start from how the loss depends on the

106
00:03:44.492 --> 00:03:45.648
current state, h t.

107
00:03:45.978 --> 00:03:48.290
Then multiply by one factor for each step

108
00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.767
back: how each state depends on the one

109
00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:51.317
before it.

110
00:03:51.791 --> 00:03:53.767
From the last slot of a median song back

111
00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:55.743
to slot zero, that's one thousand five

112
00:03:55.743 --> 00:03:57.815
hundred and ninety-nine factors, and all

113
00:03:57.815 --> 00:03:59.839
sixteen hundred states have to be kept in

114
00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.237
memory for the backward pass.

115
00:04:01.526 --> 00:04:03.502
The team estimated that whole songs at a

116
00:04:03.502 --> 00:04:05.285
batch of thirty-two would need about

117
00:04:05.285 --> 00:04:07.309
eighteen gigabytes for the LSTM and sixty

118
00:04:07.309 --> 00:04:09.092
for S4D, on a sixteen-gigabyte Mac.

119
00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:10.682
Too long, and too costly.

120
00:04:12.088 --> 00:04:14.234
So after each step the trainer detaches

121
00:04:14.234 --> 00:04:14.770
the state.

122
00:04:15.092 --> 00:04:17.291
Detach keeps the numbers but forgets how

123
00:04:17.291 --> 00:04:18.096
they were made.

124
00:04:18.418 --> 00:04:20.456
The values flow forward into the next

125
00:04:20.456 --> 00:04:22.763
chunk; the gradient stops at the edge.

126
00:04:23.085 --> 00:04:25.338
This is truncated backpropagation through

127
00:04:25.338 --> 00:04:25.606
time.

128
00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:27.889
Draw the song again.

129
00:04:28.194 --> 00:04:29.618
The values cross every edge.

130
00:04:29.924 --> 00:04:32.010
The gradients run back only to the start

131
00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:32.976
of their own chunk.

132
00:04:33.411 --> 00:04:35.737
Checked on the same real song: the

133
00:04:35.737 --> 00:04:37.947
gradient of chunk one's loss on chunk

134
00:04:37.947 --> 00:04:40.216
zero's inputs is exactly zero with the

135
00:04:40.216 --> 00:04:41.437
detach, as trained.

136
00:04:41.786 --> 00:04:44.229
Without it, about a half, summed over

137
00:04:44.229 --> 00:04:46.032
chunk zero, for L thirty-two.

138
00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:49.758
To put reach into music, the slot length.

139
00:04:50.072 --> 00:04:52.531
A slot is a sixteenth note: sixty seconds

140
00:04:52.531 --> 00:04:53.890
over four times the tempo.

141
00:04:54.204 --> 00:04:56.506
At a hundred and twenty beats per minute,

142
00:04:56.506 --> 00:04:58.546
a hundred and twenty-five milliseconds.

143
00:04:59.167 --> 00:05:01.081
So gradients reach at most five hundred

144
00:05:01.081 --> 00:05:03.091
and twelve slots: thirty-two bars,

145
00:05:03.091 --> 00:05:04.000
sixty-four seconds.

146
00:05:04.287 --> 00:05:05.388
Memory reaches further.

147
00:05:05.675 --> 00:05:07.636
The transformer's window is two thousand

148
00:05:07.636 --> 00:05:09.646
and forty-eight slots: a hundred and

149
00:05:09.646 --> 00:05:11.656
twenty-eight bars, four minutes sixteen.

150
00:05:11.943 --> 00:05:13.809
The LSTM and S4D carry from the song's

151
00:05:13.809 --> 00:05:15.531
first slot, with no window at all.

152
00:05:15.818 --> 00:05:17.780
How fast that memory fades is topic four.

153
00:05:18.177 --> 00:05:20.329
The price, in the contract's words: short

154
00:05:20.329 --> 00:05:22.350
chunks teach matching; long chunks teach

155
00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.448
matching and persistence.

156
00:05:23.712 --> 00:05:25.337
The contract measured sections about

157
00:05:25.337 --> 00:05:27.182
fourteen windows apart, so a chorus and

158
00:05:27.182 --> 00:05:29.159
its return sit roughly twenty-eight apart,

159
00:05:29.159 --> 00:05:31.092
eight hundred and ninety-six slots, so no

160
00:05:31.092 --> 00:05:32.190
gradient ever links them.

161
00:05:32.453 --> 00:05:34.430
The model can carry a chorus that far, but

162
00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:36.231
nothing in training rewards it for doing

163
00:05:36.231 --> 00:05:36.363
so.

164
00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:41.509
The trainer has a second mode, set by one

165
00:05:41.509 --> 00:05:43.167
flag, horizon slots: horizon mode.

166
00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:45.310
Chunk mode is what we've seen: thirty-two

167
00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:46.968
songs by five hundred and twelve slots a

168
00:05:46.968 --> 00:05:47.170
step.

169
00:05:47.412 --> 00:05:48.949
Horizon mode keeps the same budget,

170
00:05:48.949 --> 00:05:50.364
sixteen thousand three hundred and

171
00:05:50.364 --> 00:05:52.103
eighty-four slots a step, but walks each

172
00:05:52.103 --> 00:05:53.518
song in pieces of two thousand and

173
00:05:53.518 --> 00:05:55.216
forty-eight, with the state carried and

174
00:05:55.216 --> 00:05:56.429
detached at each piece's edge.

175
00:05:56.672 --> 00:05:58.370
Fewer songs a step, and gradients reach

176
00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.987
two thousand and forty-eight slots back.

177
00:06:00.838 --> 00:06:03.332
What changes, from the runs' own records:

178
00:06:03.332 --> 00:06:05.571
about nine songs still playing in a step,

179
00:06:05.571 --> 00:06:07.607
against about twenty-five in chunk mode.

180
00:06:07.912 --> 00:06:09.083
Each step takes longer.

181
00:06:09.388 --> 00:06:11.475
The timing pass measured L thirty-two at

182
00:06:11.475 --> 00:06:13.562
forty-nine milliseconds a step against L

183
00:06:13.562 --> 00:06:15.292
one twenty-eight at a hundred and

184
00:06:15.292 --> 00:06:17.532
twenty-one, and S thirty-two at a hundred

185
00:06:17.532 --> 00:06:19.669
and forty-four against S one twenty-eight

186
00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:21.298
at two hundred and seventy-nine.

187
00:06:21.603 --> 00:06:23.537
And the gradient clip of lesson three

188
00:06:23.537 --> 00:06:25.573
point three cut fifty-four percent of S

189
00:06:25.573 --> 00:06:27.405
one twenty-eight's steps, against

190
00:06:27.405 --> 00:06:29.390
twenty-three percent of S thirty-two's.

191
00:06:29.695 --> 00:06:31.884
The LSTMs almost never clipped: L one

192
00:06:31.884 --> 00:06:32.953
twenty-eight, once.

193
00:06:33.365 --> 00:06:34.634
Which reported runs used it?

194
00:06:34.906 --> 00:06:36.945
L one twenty-eight, S one twenty-eight and

195
00:06:36.945 --> 00:06:38.033
S one twenty-eight full.

196
00:06:38.305 --> 00:06:40.072
The numbers in those names are bars of

197
00:06:40.072 --> 00:06:40.752
gradient reach.

198
00:06:41.023 --> 00:06:43.063
T one twenty-eight, the genre drummers and

199
00:06:43.063 --> 00:06:44.966
the feedback drummer all trained in chunk

200
00:06:44.966 --> 00:06:45.192
mode.

201
00:06:45.464 --> 00:06:47.367
Careful with T one twenty-eight: its

202
00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:49.271
hundred and twenty-eight is its attention

203
00:06:49.271 --> 00:06:49.588
window.

204
00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.536
Its gradients reach five hundred and

205
00:06:51.536 --> 00:06:51.853
